Hello,


At this time we do not support .pef files.


This may be possible in the future but this is the first we have been 
made aware of them.



It would not be something we can tackle immediately.

I can try to find a workaround in the meantime if you attach a .pef file 
for me to look at.

Thanks.

Don
bOn 3/2/2022 11:09 AM, Jens Sundgaard wrote:
>
> Hi Duxbury Support
>
> At IBOS (The Institute for the Blind and Partially Sighted) in Denmark 
> we have developed the "IBOS MusicXmlReader" which can convert standard 
> MusicXml files into Music Braille.
>
> The Music Braille information is:
>
>   * Generated according to the rules in the BANA 2015 specification in
>     http://www.brailleauthority.org/music/Music_Braille_Code_2015.pdf
>   * Stored in a .pef file, as specified in
>     http://braillespecs.github.io/pef/pef-specification.html
>
> Two of our  users: NOTA and DBS (both of them are Danish organisations 
> supporting the blind and partially sighted)  use identical "Braille 
> 650 SF2" high speed embossers but use different software:
>
>   * NOTA uses a home-made conversion tool for transferring the .pef
>     files to the Braillo embosser. This works apparently fine.
>   * DBS uses Duxbury DBT and can not find a way to transfer the .pef
>     files correctly to the embosser.
>
> I am trying to help DBS and have installed a DEMO version of DBS 12.5 
> SR4 on my PC.
>
> First, in "Global", "Embosser Setup" I select Brailleo 650 SF becase I 
> need the DTB functionality for handling this specific Norwegian printer.
>
> Then, after opening a .pef file I look for in the Input Filter for a 
> filter named ".pef" (or something similar) but it does not exist.
>
> So my questions are:
>
>  1. Does DBT support the .pef file format ?
>  2. If "Yes": How do I open a .pef file as input in "DBT"?
>  3. If "No": Can you suggest any alternatives?
>
> What I need is a simple "dot by dot" mapping of the contents of the 
> .pef file where the Music Braille information is already fully 
> formatted in rows and columns as Unicode characters in the Unicode 
> Braille interval 0x2800 to 0x283F.
>
> No conversion is required, except the trivial removal of the XML 
> notation in the .pef file.
>
> I hope you can help me and DBS here!
>
> Best regards
>
> Jens Sundgaard,
>
> IBOS, Denmark
